#: ../../source/glob-to-regexp_README.md:5
msgid ""
"Turn a \\*-wildcard style glob (`\"*.min.js\"`) into a regular expression"
" (`/^.*\\.min\\.js$/`)!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/glob-to-regexp_README.md:8
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"To match bash-like globs, eg. `?` for any single-character match, `[a-z]`"
" for character ranges, and `{*.html, *.js}` for multiple alternatives, "
"call with `{ extended: true }`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/glob-to-regexp_README.md:12
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"To obey [globstars `**`](https://github.com/isaacs/node-glob#glob-primer)"
" rules set option `{globstar: true}`. NOTE: This changes the behavior of "
"`*` when `globstar` is `true` as shown below: When `{globstar: true}`: "
"`/foo/**` will match any string that starts with `/foo/` like "
"`/foo/index.htm`, `/foo/bar/baz.txt`, etc.  Also, `/foo/**/*.txt` will "
"match any string that starts with `/foo/` and ends with `.txt` like "
"`/foo/bar.txt`, `/foo/bar/baz.txt`, etc. Whereas `/foo/*` (single `*`, "
"not a globstar) will match strings that start with `/foo/` like "
"`/foo/index.htm`, `/foo/baz.txt` but will not match strings that contain "
"a `/` to the right like `/foo/bar/baz.txt`, `/foo/bar/baz/qux.dat`, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/glob-to-regexp_README.md:22
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Set flags on the resulting `RegExp` object by adding the `flags` property"
" to the option object, eg `{ flags: \"i\" }` for ignoring case."
msgstr ""

